#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# is_primitive_polynomial

#% text
# `is_primitive_polynomial(f, p)` gives `true` when the polynomial `f` is primitive modulo the prime `p`: irreducible, with a root that generates the multiplicative group of the field it defines.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Equivalently `x` has order `p^n - 1` modulo `f` for `f` of degree `n`. The test needs the factorisation of `p^n - 1`; when it cannot be found the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
is_primitive_polynomial(x^4 + x + 1, 2)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
is_primitive_polynomial(x^4 + x^3 + x^2 + x + 1, 2)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The second polynomial is irreducible, but its roots have order 5:

#% cell
is_irreducible_polynomial(x^4 + x^3 + x^2 + x + 1, modulus: 2)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `is_irreducible_polynomial`, `conway_polynomial`, `is_finite_field_element_primitive`
